Iface as a struct field — value struct returned from factory
=============================================================

`iface_box_lifetime.lsysl::test_sibl_iface_field_in_struct`
pins the in-scope case (`val c = Tally(40); val w =
Wrapper(c, 2)`). This file pins the **factory-return**
case where a value struct carrying an iface field must
survive the factory's frame exit.

Companion bug to `iface_in_option_result.lsysl`'s
return-from-fn case: the struct ctor's iface auto-box
needed `owns = true` (it was missing) so the iface
descriptor's data buffer is heap-malloc'd instead of
pointing at the factory's stack slot. Both non-generic
struct ctor and generic struct ctor paths were patched —
see `SyslAnalyzerExpressions.scala` near "Struct
constructor".

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    interface PsiV
        value() -> int

    struct PsiP
        n: int

    PsiP.value() -> int = self.n

    struct PsiWrap
        h: PsiV

    psi_factory(n: int) -> PsiWrap
        val src = PsiP(n)
        return PsiWrap(src)

Single factory call — iface field reads correct value
-----------------------------------------------------

The factory builds `PsiWrap(src)` where src is a local
struct. The iface field's data buffer must be heap-copied
so it outlives the factory's frame.

    #test
    test_struct_with_iface_field_returned() -> unit
        val w = psi_factory(42)
        assert(w.h.value() == 42, s"expected 42, got ${w.h.value()}")

Two factory calls — no aliasing
-------------------------------

Both calls allocate their own heap data buffer for the
iface field. Without the owns=true fix, both ifaces
aliased the same reused stack slot (second value
overwrote first).

    #test
    test_two_struct_with_iface_field_returned() -> unit
        val a = psi_factory(10)
        val b = psi_factory(20)
        val va = a.h.value()
        val vb = b.h.value()
        assert(va == 10, s"va: expected 10, got $va")
        assert(vb == 20, s"vb: expected 20, got $vb")

Three calls — earliest survives later allocations
-------------------------------------------------

Read the first call's value AFTER making two more calls.
Without the fix this read sees a later value because the
heap buffer is shared.

    #test
    test_struct_with_iface_field_three_calls() -> unit
        val w = psi_factory(7)
        val a = psi_factory(8)
        val b = psi_factory(9)
        assert(w.h.value() == 7, s"w preserved: expected 7, got ${w.h.value()}")
        assert(a.h.value() == 8, s"a: expected 8, got ${a.h.value()}")
        assert(b.h.value() == 9, s"b: expected 9, got ${b.h.value()}")

Struct ctor inline (no factory) — still works
---------------------------------------------

The owns=true change also applies to in-scope use of
struct constructors with iface fields. Stays correct.

    #test
    test_struct_with_iface_field_inline() -> unit
        val src = PsiP(99)
        val w = PsiWrap(src)
        assert(w.h.value() == 99, s"inline: expected 99, got ${w.h.value()}")

Generic struct with iface field, explicit type arg
--------------------------------------------------

`PsiGenericWrap[PsiV](src, t)` exercises the explicit-
type-arg generic struct ctor path (`GenericCallAST` /
`IndexAST` → `genericStructs`). The iface field auto-box
needed owns=true.

    struct PsiGenericWrap[T]
        h: T
        tag: int

    psi_generic_factory_explicit(n: int, t: int) -> PsiGenericWrap[PsiV]
        val src = PsiP(n)
        return PsiGenericWrap[PsiV](src, t)

    #test
    test_generic_struct_explicit_typearg_returned() -> unit
        val a = psi_generic_factory_explicit(10, 1)
        val b = psi_generic_factory_explicit(20, 2)
        assert(a.h.value() == 10, s"a: expected 10, got ${a.h.value()}")
        assert(b.h.value() == 20, s"b: expected 20, got ${b.h.value()}")
        assert(a.tag == 1, s"a.tag: ${a.tag}")
        assert(b.tag == 2, s"b.tag: ${b.tag}")

Generic struct with iface field, inferred type arg
--------------------------------------------------

`PsiGenericWrap(src, t)` infers T from the args. Exercises
the inferred-type-arg path in the analyzer's struct-ctor
case (the `genericStructs.contains(name)` branch in
`CallAST` analysis). Needs the field type to drive an
iface coercion from the concrete struct.

    psi_generic_factory_inferred(n: int, t: int) -> PsiGenericWrap[PsiV]
        val src: PsiV = PsiP(n)
        return PsiGenericWrap(src, t)

    #test
    test_generic_struct_inferred_typearg_returned() -> unit
        val a = psi_generic_factory_inferred(30, 3)
        val b = psi_generic_factory_inferred(40, 4)
        assert(a.h.value() == 30, s"a: expected 30, got ${a.h.value()}")
        assert(b.h.value() == 40, s"b: expected 40, got ${b.h.value()}")
        assert(a.tag == 3, s"a.tag: ${a.tag}")
        assert(b.tag == 4, s"b.tag: ${b.tag}")
